UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 1: Where food comes from', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Food comes from plants and animals (and fungi in some foods). Tracing a meal back to farms, gardens or oceans shows its sources.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nTrace: hamburger bun; lettuce; beef patty. Plant or animal source?\n\nFEEDBACK\nBun plant (wheat); lettuce plant; patty animal (cattle) — note some patties are plant-based if labelled.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose a real meal. Map each item to plant/animal/other source. Draw a simple farm-to-plate arrow for three items.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Food comes from plants and animals (and fungi in some foods). Tracing a meal back to farms, gardens or oceans shows its sources.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose a real meal. Map each item to plant/animal/other source. Draw a simple farm-to-plate arrow for three items. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about food linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science.. Correction: Bun plant (wheat); lettuce plant; patty animal (cattle) — note some patties are plant-based if labelled. with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses food. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about food, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about food. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for food. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for food. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Bun plant (wheat); lettuce plant; patty animal (cattle) — note some patties are plant-based if labelled","Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Trace: hamburger bun; lettuce; beef patty. Plant or animal source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for food; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Food comes from plants and animals (and fungi in some foods)","Bun plant (wheat); lettuce plant; patty animal (cattle) — note some patties are plant-based if labelled","Careful use of farm and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with food?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named farm and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science","Name farm and explain why it matters only, without linking it to food","Apply food to new material and name the farm and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies food with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone","Decide from which object looks most exciting","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation","Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science"],"question":"New examples: a charging phone, a nesting bird, a glass marble, a fallen dry leaf. Which claim about food is most trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A named observation plus how it supports the claim","A longer story with no observation","A label copied from the title with no evidence","Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about food?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about food with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Bun plant (wheat); lettuce plant; patty animal (cattle) — note some patties are plant-based if labelled","Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science"],"question":"Using this science idea — Food comes from plants and animals (and fungi in some foods). — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about food?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Any exciting story about the object must be true","Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science","Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded","If the observed feature changes, the claim about food should change with it"],"question":"Which prediction about food can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about food. Reject title-only labels with no observation.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses food. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about food linked to that evidence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for food. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about food, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science.. Correction: Bun plant (wheat); lettuce plant; patty animal (cattle) — note some patties are plant-based if labelled. with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y3-w31-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise food.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Agriculture and Food Technologies","success_criteria":["I can use food with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify farm and explain why it matters.","I can practise source independently on a new prompt.","I can notice plant/animal needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Food comes from plants and animals (and fungi in some foods).","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Food comes from plants and animals (and fungi in some foods)"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about food with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBread ← wheat plants. Cheese ← milk ← cows/goats. Apple ← apple tree. Fish ← ocean/river catch or farmed fish.\n\nTRY FIRST\nTrace: hamburger bun; lettuce; beef patty. Plant or animal source?\n\nFEEDBACK\nBun plant (wheat); lettuce plant; patty animal (cattle) — note some patties are plant-based if labelled.","explain":"Food comes from plants and animals (and fungi in some foods). Tracing a meal back to farms, gardens or oceans shows its sources.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Food sources.","caption":"Supermarket is not the origin.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Plant foods</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">wheat apple lettuce</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Animal foods</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">milk meat eggs</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Bread ← wheat plants. Cheese ← milk ← cows/goats. Apple ← apple tree. Fish ← ocean/river catch or farmed fish. Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Bread ← wheat plants. Cheese ← milk ← cows/goats. Apple ← apple tree. Fish ← ocean/river catch or farmed fish. Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about food with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBread ← wheat plants. Cheese ← milk ← cows/goats. Apple ← apple tree. Fish ← ocean/river catch or farmed fish. Look at each observation, variable or design choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Tracing a meal.","caption":"Three items mapped.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Meal map</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">item → source</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Arrows</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">farm to plate</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about food with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science. Here is a more productive model: Bread ← wheat plants. Cheese ← milk ← cows/goats. Apple ← apple tree. Fish ← ocean/river catch or farmed fish.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nBun plant (wheat); lettuce plant; patty animal (cattle) — note some patties are plant-based if labelled.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking food “comes from the supermarket” stops one step too soon for science. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about food with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nFood comes from plants and animals (and fungi in some foods). Tracing a meal back to farms, gardens or oceans shows its sources. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInvestigate or describe one fresh example that uses the 